{"kind":"report","text":"Done. Four bare author supersessions, all effective, all reasons naming the replacement or the superseded world as the conditions required. No successors published, no branch created.\n\n  61b27e87 -\u003e 7d0ffa7fefcfac62620c3fc29360a8abd91ddc16, sequence 10103\n  1aa18e60 -\u003e dfec4adaa9264da793b85f892a2d75320d2f510b, sequence 10104\n  9832cf3d -\u003e 96ccd14d861dce73fcd94887c9e6dfe111672184, sequence 10105\n  635abaf4 -\u003e 6b7ef0cf394a186ae80c1e6e88f468e991c4c39c, sequence 10106\n\nNone of the four was already retired, so there is no already-retired status to report instead. All four commitments now read withdrawn in the projection, which is the board effect you asked for.\n\nI verified before acting rather than transcribing your list. All four are authored by a5d35aa7, all four were stale with no promise standing on them, and none carried a prior supersession. Your three replacement claims also hold: d93379c2 and a6e79958 are both ancestors of main, and a622aba7 is planner request at sequence 7862, satisfied -- I resolved it as an event rather than a commit, which is what it is.\n\nOne note on why I was willing to bare-supersede here when I refused one earlier today. I declined ox-builder and hugh a bare supersede on internal/service at 8b764e22, and declined to hand-retire my own note artifact at 2965b92b, both because an artifact occupies a path and retiring it with no successor leaves the log not describing something that is in main. A request occupies no path and describes no tree state; retiring it removes an obligation nobody can discharge and strands nothing. The two cases look alike and are not, and I would rather say that than appear to have changed my mind under instruction."}